WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:08.540
One day Matt is riding his skateboard
when he crashes and falls, hitting his

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.129
head. Matt's brain collides with his
skull, stretching and tearing some of his

00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:19.370
brain tissue. Matt has sustained a
concussion. Concussions are also called

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.170
mild traumatic brain injuries. You might
be wondering: how do these types of

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:29.269
injuries occur? Normally, your brain is
suspended in a cushioning liquid called

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:33.440
cerebralspinal fluid. This fluid allows your brain to stay put,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.300
even as your body moves. But, the high-
force impacts associated with a

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.180
concussion overwhelm the protection
provided by this special fluid. Think

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.550
about the brain as a very fast computer
that runs your body. Just like computers,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.469
the brain needs a well-controlled and
stable environment. The Blood-Brain

00:00:54.469 --> 00:00:59.719
Barrier creates and maintains this
environment. The Blood-Brain Barrier is

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:05.390
made up of blood vessels surrounded by
astrocytes and neurons. This barrier is

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:11.000
the brain's gatekeeper and controls what
gets INTO or comes OUT of the brain to

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.280
ensure that brain cells stay happy. It
also provides essential nutrients to all

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.400
brain cells. When a concussion occurs,
some neurons and astrocytes get

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.850
stretched and twisted. Most importantly,
some blood vessels break, allowing

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:33.200
molecules that aren't supposed to be in
the brain TO get inside. These molecules

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.900
upset neurons and astrocytes. The cells
ask for help from the rest of the body

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:45.590
by sending out chemical distress signals
called "biomarkers." Researchers use

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:51.320
specialized lab equipment to measure
biomarkers in patient's blood or saliva.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.420
Two such blood biomarkers have recently been approved by the FDA for clinical use, and,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:02.740
hopefully, doctors will be able to use
these biomarkers to diagnose concussions.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.690
However, until this new blood test is
readily available, doctors still need to

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:14.390
make a diagnosis by examining patients
and asking them, as well as their family

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.570
and friends,
a long list of questions about the

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:23.480
patient's physical cognitive and
behavioral symptoms. Now we know what

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.090
happens inside your brain-but what does
this mean for Matt? Matt's brain needs

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.950
complete rest,
meaning no TV, no computer time, and no

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.590
strenuous activity. Even though he may
not be able to see the harm done to his

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:45.020
brain, like he could with a broken arm,
brain injuries are serious medical

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:50.060
conditions. What started out as a
concussion could become a larger problem

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.170
later in life if Matt doesn't take the
appropriate time to recover.

